<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pachuca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pachuca de Soto</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pachuca is the capital of the state of Hidalgo in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. The nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-de-las-navajas">Sierra de las Navajas</a> was a major source of green obsidian, worked by Teotihuacan and the Toltecs, and from the 16th century the district became one of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Cornish miners who came in the 19th century are credited with introducing football to Mexico there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pachuca.jpg" alt="Pachuca" width="320" height="567" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Microstar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Montaje_de_Pachuca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pachuca, formally Pachuca de Soto, is the capital and largest city of the state of Hidalgo, about 90 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. It lies in a small valley almost surrounded by hills, and its nickname, La Bella Airosa, refers to the strong winds that blow through the canyons to the north. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> language it is Nju̱nthe. Its official name honours Manuel Fernando Soto, a congressman credited with founding the state, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> declared it the capital of Hidalgo in 1869. The origin of the word pachuca is not agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People mined green obsidian in the Sierra de Pachuca, at Cerro de las Navajas, as early as 12,000 BCE. Artifacts from between 200 and 850 CE show Teotihuacan influence, and it has been argued that Teotihuacan monopolised the deposit to control the obsidian trade of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. The obsidian was prized in pre-Columbian times and traded as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiro-mounds">Spiro Mounds</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Spanish began mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> here in the mid-16th century, and the city remained a major mining centre until the mid-20th century.</p><p>From 1824 Cornish miners and English investors came to Pachuca and nearby Real del Monte; some founded the Compañía Real de Monte y Pachuca. Ships carrying 125 passengers and about 1,500 tons of equipment sailed from Falmouth and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> three months later, and the trek inland killed about half the miners and their families, many from malaria and yellow fever. The survivors brought high-pressure steam pumping engines of the kind designed by Richard Trevithick, which turned flooded mines into large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> producers. They also brought football and the pasty, known in Pachuca as pastes, which became defining features of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A manufacturing sector, set up in the 1950s, has changed the city's mining image. The last Pachuca mine to close was El Álamo, in 1993. The Universidad Autónoma del Estado de Hidalgo, the state's oldest educational institution, began in 1869 as the Instituto Literario y Escuela de Artes y Oficios. The Monumental Clock, 40 metres high, was donated by the Cornishman Francis Rule and inaugurated on 15 September 1910 for the centennial of Mexican independence. The English Cemetery holds about 600 graves, mostly of Cornish miners and their families, and many of their descendants still live in the area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pachuca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Silver transformed the economy after the discoveries at Zacatecas in 1546 and Guanajuato soon after; the patio process of amalgamation with mercury, developed by Bartolomé de Medina at <b>Pachuca</b> in the 1550s, made mass production possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Workshops turned obsidian from the nearby Otumba sources and the green obsidian of the Sierra de las Navajas near <b>Pachuca</b> into blades and points, and green Teotihuacan obsidian travelled widely across Mesoamerica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Workshops turned obsidian, including the prized green obsidian of the Sierra de las Navajas near <b>Pachuca</b>, into blades and tools, and Tula became a major centre of the obsidian trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
